Core Concept
The thin threads pulled from Aurora don’t travel through open air or mystical ley lines. They run through the city’s actual power infrastructure — the “electric lines” that light streets, run lifts, keep the deep pumps going, and feed the civic buildings.
To almost everyone, the Weavers are simply the people who keep the lights on and the machinery running. They wear the practical gear, carry the right tools, and have access to tunnels and conduits no one else bothers with. Their work is treated as mundane municipal maintenance.
In reality, a core part of what they maintain is the extraction grid.
How It Works
The jagged black crystals with the soft purple glow are the primary nodes / transformers.
The near-invisible threads are carried inside or alongside the ordinary conductive lines.
When a Weaver opens a junction box or drops into a service tunnel, they are sometimes looking at both systems at once: the mundane current that runs the city, and the far thinner, deeper current that keeps the city together.
Most Weavers only know the official job.
A smaller circle knows that certain lines must never be cut, that certain crystals must stay at a particular glow, and that some outages are more dangerous than others.
A very small number understand (or have begun to suspect) what the deep current actually is.

Why This Is Strong
It makes the extraction infrastructural and normalized.
It gives the city a living, working class that sits dangerously close to the secret without being priests or nobles.
It creates immediate play potential: a Weaver NPC, a service tunnel the party has to move through, a “power outage” that is actually an extraction fluctuation, a junior Weaver who saw something they weren’t supposed to see.
It fits the tone perfectly — the horror is not a hidden temple. The horror is that the system that keeps the lights on is the same system that keeps Aurora bound.
Relationship to Existing Factions
Masons: Overlap in the deep infrastructure. Possible tension over who has authority in shared tunnels.
Regency / Oswald’s circle: The Weavers are a necessary utility. Some of the higher Weavers may already be quietly co-opted.
People of Promise: Would view the Weavers’ work as a particularly intimate form of wrongful holding.
Temples: Mostly ignore them until the lights fail.

**Yes. This version is stronger.**

The Weavers are not purely electricians. They are **textile workers of a very old, very large loom** that happens to use electrical infrastructure as part of its machinery.

### Combined Visual & Process

Deep beneath the city, in the sealed foundation levels, thousands of the near-invisible threads are drawn from the black crystals. Individually they are hair-thin, visible only as air ripples or faint purple-edged distortions.

At collection points the threads are gathered, sorted, and spun together into thicker working cords. This is where the electrical equipment enters:

- Arrays of tall ceramic or composite bushings, tension frames, and heavy industrial spools (exactly the visual language of the substation in the image) act as **drawing frames, tensioners, and take-up reels**.
- The equipment looks ordinary to anyone who doesn’t know what it is actually pulling.
- The soft purple glow travels along the threads as they are collected and thickened; under load the glow pulses slowly through the larger cords.

Once the threads have been combined into stable cords, the true weaving begins. In long, low chambers beneath the civic districts the Weavers work the cords into an enormous, continuous tapestry that is woven directly into the city’s foundation and utility grid. The tapestry is not decorative. It is the load-bearing structure for the extracted binding force. Sections of it run parallel to ordinary power lines, water mains, and structural supports so that the two systems are physically interleaved.

### Hierarchy Implications (Updated)

- **Line Hands** mostly work the upper electrical layer and only rarely see the thicker cords.
- **Junction / Tunnel Crews** move between the electrical gear and the early collection frames. They handle the spools and tension equipment.
- **Line Keepers** supervise both the electrical side and the spinning of thin threads into cords.
- **Threadwrights** are the actual weavers of the deep tapestry. They decide pattern density, repair breaks in the weave, and know how different sections of the city are “held” by different parts of the work.
- The Quiet Chair is the only one who routinely walks the entire loom — from crystal to finished tapestry — and understands what the finished work is doing to Aurora and to the city.

### Tone Strength

This keeps the best of both ideas:

- The electrical infrastructure makes the system look modern, municipal, and easy to ignore.
- The weaving craft makes it ancient, intimate, and irreversible. A tapestry cannot simply be switched off. It has to be unwoven.
- The image of giant industrial spools and bushings acting as loom components is quietly perfect — the same objects that should just be moving power are also winding up something that was never meant to be yarn.

The Weavers are now a proper craft guild with a secret second trade: they keep the lights on, and they keep the city bound.

